<root BTCPP_format="4">
  <!--
    Plan a path, follow it, and replan about once a second while following.

    PipelineSequence keeps FollowPath running while RateController re-ticks
    ComputePathToPose at 1 Hz. Each new plan replaces {path} and starts from the
    robot's current position, and FollowPath switches to it mid-drive. Each plan
    also reads the current {goal}, so a goal published while the robot is already
    driving takes effect at the next replan. The global costmap holds only the
    static map and its inflation, so replanning does not route around anything
    new: it re-anchors the path to where the robot actually is, toward whichever
    goal is current.

    ComputePathToPose is wrapped in a retry because its failure mode here is not
    "no route exists" — it is "planner_server did not acknowledge the request in
    time". That is a transient timing race against a loaded host, and retrying it
    costs one extra round trip. Without the retry a single missed acknowledgement
    fails the PipelineSequence, which fails the tree, which aborts the whole
    navigation goal. Two robots were stranded that way on 2026-08-11, when the
    tree still planned once, up front. With replanning the planner is asked again
    every second, so the same failure can now abort a goal at any point in a drive.

    Recovery *behaviors* (spin, back up, wait) are still deliberately absent: they
    would need a behavior_server, which multi_nav2_launch.py does not start. A
    retry decorator is plain behavior-tree logic and needs no extra node.
  -->
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainBT">
    <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
      <RateController hz="1.0">
        <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="3">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
        </RetryUntilSuccessful>
      </RateController>
      <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
    </PipelineSequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
